menu "airmidi board"

    choice AIRMIDI_BOARD
        prompt "Board"
        default AIRMIDI_BOARD_XIAO_C6 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6
        default AIRMIDI_BOARD_XIAO_S3 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
        help
            The receiver PCB (rev A) with the XIAO module it carries. Selects the pin table and the board init.

        config AIRMIDI_BOARD_XIAO_C6
            bool "receiver rev A + XIAO ESP32-C6"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6

        config AIRMIDI_BOARD_XIAO_S3
            bool "receiver rev A + XIAO ESP32-S3"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3

        config AIRMIDI_BOARD_DEVKITC_S3
            bool "ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 on its own"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
            help
                A bare dev kit on its native USB port: USB MIDI, the radios and the web UI, no TRS jacks wired
                (UART MIDI on GPIO17/18 if you add them), no battery, the BOOT button as the box's button.
    endchoice

    config AIRMIDI_USB_HOST
        bool "Read a USB MIDI device on the USB port (host role)"
        default n
        depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
        help
            The S3's one USB port normally presents a USB MIDI device to a computer, with the console on a
            serial port beside it. In host role it reads a device instead: a digital piano that has neither
            5-pin DIN nor Bluetooth is a USB MIDI device (its USB TO HOST port), and this is what lets the
            box take its notes and pedals and send them out over BLE and Wi-Fi, so nothing has to be
            tethered to a tablet. One port, one role: this build has no USB console and no USB MIDI
            output, and the console stays on UART0, so the dev kit's USB-UART bridge port carries the log.

            The piano needs a real host adapter: see AIRMIDI_USB_HOST_VBUS_GPIO.

    config AIRMIDI_USB_HOST_VBUS_GPIO
        int "VBUS enable GPIO"
        default 4
        depends on AIRMIDI_USB_HOST
        help
            A USB host has to raise VBUS itself; the port supplies none. Drive this GPIO through whatever
            switches 5 V onto the piano's cable (a USB host adapter or a USB-A break-out has a VBUS pin for
            this). Without it the piano's USB transceiver does not power up and no device ever appears.

endmenu
